scythe@u.washington.edu (The Grim Reaper) writes:


>I agree.  Elves shouldn't be classified as chaotic, anyway.  Perhaps if
>the Dev Team is just rying to keep the alignments balanced, Barbarians
>should be moved to chaotic, and elves to neutral.  But it would be cool to
>be able to pick an alignment at the start.  I'd think most classes could
>pick from all three, but some would have restrictions.  Knights, for instance,
>could be lawful or chaotic (the black knight), but not neutral.  Rogues
>couldn't be lawful.  Valkyries probably shouldn't be lawful either.  
>Samurai should probably have to be lawful.  Any other limitations?

I think there should be a fair number of limitations, to make it
more interesting.  This is purely off the top of my head, according
to what seems appropriate to me:

Class             Allowed Alignments
----              ------------------
Archaeologist      N
Barbarian          NC
Caveman           LNC
Elf               LNC
Healer            LN
Knight            L C
Priest            L C
Rogue               C
Samurai           L
Tourist           LNC
Valkyrie           NC
Wizard            LNC

Only four classes (Caveman, Elf, Tourist, and Wizard) can choose from
all three alignments.  Three classes (Samurai, Archaeologist, and Rogue)
are limited to a single alignment.  (I know in the current game
Archaeologists are Lawful, but for them to be Neutral makes more
sense to me--I've always thought of the Arch. as someone who would
go exploring a site regardless of what local authorities said, for
example...)  Knights (as the previous poster mentioned) as well as
Priests can be Lawful or Chaotic, but not Neutral.

This is also still fairly balanced-- 8 classes may be Lawful, 8 may
be Neutral, and 9 may be chaotic.  (If you feel that balance is really
important, take away the Chaotic option from the Tourist.)


